# 57. Artemis Memory, Learning, Advisor Performance and Continuous Improvement

Artemis is intended to improve over time, but “learning” must be defined in a
way that is measurable, reproducible and safe.

Artemis must not claim that it is learning merely because more chat history or
more model responses exist.

## 57.1 Memory Classes

Artemis may maintain distinct memory classes, each with one canonical owner and
retention policy:

### Decision / Episodic Memory

- what context existed
- what evidence was available
- what decisions were made
- what advisors contributed
- what controls accepted or rejected
- what later outcome occurred

### Knowledge / Semantic Memory

- verified reusable facts
- canonical definitions
- approved domain knowledge
- provider capabilities
- system architecture knowledge
- lessons derived from validated outcomes

### Evaluation Memory

- Agent performance
- advisor performance
- calibration
- failure patterns
- regime-specific quality
- latency/cost observations
- abstention/refusal quality

### Operational Memory

- incidents
- recovery actions
- provider outages
- degraded modes
- known recurring operational patterns

Do not collapse all memory into one uncontrolled vector store or one free-form
conversation history.

## 57.2 Memory Provenance and Freshness

Every reusable memory item should support, where applicable:

- memoryId
- source type
- source reference
- creation time
- source time
- last verified time
- expiry or review time
- confidence/provenance class
- domain
- scope
- privacy classification
- version
- superseded-by relationship

A model-generated statement must not be promoted to factual memory without the
required verification.

A prediction must not become “knowledge” merely because Artemis produced it.

Future observed outcomes must be linked separately.

## 57.3 Advisor Performance Is Contextual

External advisors must not receive one permanent global trust score.

Performance should be segmented where meaningful by:

- provider/model version
- task type
- domain
- market
- symbol
- timeframe
- horizon
- regime
- language
- tool/retrieval availability
- prompt/policy version

Evaluation may measure different dimensions, including:

- factual correctness
- citation/source quality
- forecast calibration
- directional usefulness
- risk identification
- contradiction detection
- uncertainty quality
- abstention quality
- robustness under adversarial critique
- consistency
- latency
- cost efficiency

Do not reduce all advisor quality to one “accuracy” number.

## 57.4 Teacher → Peer → Specialist Evolution

The intended long-term relationship with external AI systems may evolve through:

### Teacher / Strong Advisor

Artemis consults external models frequently because internal capability is not
yet mature.

### Peer Advisor

Artemis and selected external models provide comparable independent analysis and
mutual critique.

### Selective Specialist

Artemis performs most routine synthesis itself and calls external specialists
only where their demonstrated domain value justifies the cost, latency or
privacy exposure.

### Optional Challenger / Second Opinion

Artemis is operationally self-sufficient for the approved domain but may call an
external advisor for challenge, rare expertise, incident analysis or periodic
benchmarking.

Promotion between these states must be evidence-based.

Do not assume Artemis becomes better than an external model simply because time
has passed or more data has accumulated.

## 57.5 Continuous Learning Loop

The approved learning loop is conceptually:

Observe
→ Analyze
→ Decide
→ Record
→ Observe Outcome
→ Evaluate
→ Calibrate
→ Propose Improvement
→ Validate Offline
→ Shadow
→ Promote only when approved

Possible safe improvement mechanisms include:

- better retrieval
- better memory selection
- improved task routing
- improved advisor selection
- evidence-based advisor weighting
- calibrated confidence
- prompt/policy version improvements
- feature/algorithm improvements
- approved model fine-tuning
- approved local models
- improved conflict resolution

Learning does not authorize uncontrolled self-modification.

## 57.6 No Autonomous Production Self-Modification

Artemis may identify, recommend or prepare candidate improvements, but it must
not autonomously:

- modify production code
- push Git commits
- merge PRs
- change Rule 01 or Rule 02
- change credentials
- change RBAC
- change Emergency Stop semantics
- change runtime mode
- increase position or loss limits
- change execution eligibility
- activate Live trading
- change provider permissions
- change execution-relevant policy weights
- deploy itself

Production-affecting improvements must follow the normal TitanGold engineering,
test, Human-QA, Git and deployment lifecycle.

## 57.7 Model Training / Fine-Tuning Governance

If Artemis eventually trains or fine-tunes a model, the work must be a separately
approved Program Slice.

It must define:

- training objective
- dataset owner
- dataset provenance
- licensing/usage rights where relevant
- privacy classification
- train/validation/test split
- leakage controls
- temporal split for market tasks where appropriate
- baseline model
- evaluation suite
- calibration
- regression thresholds
- version identifier
- reproducibility
- rollback
- deployment gate

Do not train on:

- Secrets
- private provider credentials
- unauthorized user data
- future outcomes that leak into historical inputs
- unverified model-generated text presented as ground truth
- duplicated evaluation data that invalidates the holdout set

A newly trained model must not replace the production model solely because its
training loss is lower.

## 57.8 Feedback-Loop Protection

Artemis must distinguish:

- external fact
- Agent-derived evidence
- external advisor opinion
- Artemis synthesis
- later observed outcome
- human evaluation

Do not recursively reinforce Artemis-generated opinions as independent evidence.

Do not count the same model-generated claim after storage/retrieval as a new
confirmation.

Do not let a model train on its own historical predictions and then evaluate
against those predictions as though they were ground truth.

