# 58. Artemis Self-Sufficiency, Governance and External-AI Safety Gates

TitanGold's long-term objective is a highly capable Artemis that can perform the
approved domain work increasingly independently while using external AI systems
selectively and intelligently.

Self-sufficiency is a measured system property, not a marketing label.

## 58.1 Self-Sufficiency Criteria

Artemis may be described as more independent only when evidence shows, for the
approved scope:

- internal Agents and memory provide sufficient coverage
- external-provider outages do not collapse the core workflow
- decision quality remains within approved thresholds without routine external
  consultation
- calibration remains acceptable
- risk detection remains acceptable
- provenance remains complete
- failure behavior remains safe
- latency and cost remain acceptable
- evaluation is segmented by domain/regime rather than hidden in one aggregate
- periodic external benchmarking does not reveal a material quality regression

Do not claim that Artemis is “better than ChatGPT”, “better than Gemini”,
“better than DeepSeek” or better than any other model globally.

Comparative claims must be:

- domain-specific
- task-specific
- version-specific
- methodology-defined
- reproducible
- time-bounded

## 58.2 External Advisors Remain Replaceable

Artemis architecture must tolerate:

- provider removal
- model deprecation
- price changes
- quota changes
- region changes
- provider outages
- model-quality regression
- policy changes

No single external AI provider should become an undocumented single point of
failure for the canonical Artemis workflow.

## 58.3 Privacy and Minimum Necessary Context

External advisors receive only the minimum context required for the approved
advisory task.

Before sending context externally, classify it as:

- public
- internal product-safe
- privileged diagnostic
- user-private
- secret / prohibited

Secret/prohibited data must never be sent.

User-private or privileged data requires an explicitly approved use case,
provider policy compatibility and the required authorization.

Do not send an entire database row, log bundle, conversation history or user
profile when a bounded derived context is sufficient.

## 58.4 Prompt-Injection and Tool Safety

Content retrieved from the web, documents, news, provider payloads or user data
must be treated as data, not trusted instructions.

External advisor prompts and tool layers must defend against:

- prompt injection
- instruction smuggling
- data exfiltration
- secret requests
- tool escalation
- malicious URLs or payloads
- arbitrary code execution
- cross-tenant data exposure

An external source cannot grant itself tool permission.

A model cannot authorize its own elevation.

## 58.5 Human Governance and Explainability

For material Artemis changes, the system must preserve enough explanation for a
human reviewer to understand:

- what changed
- why it changed
- which evidence supported it
- which advisors influenced it
- which evaluation improved
- which risks remain
- how to roll back

Human QA remains final authority at the required milestone.

## 58.6 Relationship to Automated-Trading Maturity

The Multi-AI Council, memory, advisor scoring, improved routing or Artemis
self-sufficiency do not independently advance the trading maturity stage.

Section 53 remains authoritative.

External AI advice can never bypass:

- Risk veto
- Portfolio bounds
- Liquidity feasibility
- runtime safety
- Emergency Stop
- provider capability checks
- confirmation rules
- Order Management idempotency
- Tier-4 authorization

A more intelligent Artemis must also be a more traceable and better-governed
Artemis.

